---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE, SERIES } from '../../consts';

const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About this collection',
  description: 'How these essays are researched and written, their scope, changelog and licence.',
  url: new URL(SITE.base + '/en/about/', Astro.site).toString(),
  inLanguage: 'en',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: SITE.author,
    alternateName: SITE.authorEn,
    url: SITE.personalSite,
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About this collection"
  description="How these essays are researched and written, their scope, changelog and licence."
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <div class="shell page-hero">
    <p class="label">About / Methodology</p>
    <h1>About this collection</h1>
    <p>
      One engineer's writing space: following a timeline and a set of technical questions to spell out
      what I have built, what I have read, and which potholes I fell into. This page covers how the
      writing works, where its data comes from, and what changed when.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="shell about-page-grid">
    <aside class="about-side">
      <ul class="info-list">
        <li><dt>Author</dt><dd>{SITE.author}</dd></li>
        <li><dt>Structure</dt><dd>1 series + 2 essays</dd></li>
        <li><dt>Chapters</dt><dd>{SERIES.chapterCount}</dd></li>
        <li><dt>Span</dt><dd>{SERIES.start} — {SERIES.end}</dd></li>
        <li><dt>Last update</dt><dd>2026.10.27</dd></li>
        <li><dt>Licence</dt><dd>{SERIES.license}</dd></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <div class="about-prose">
      <h2>Why write</h2>
      <p>
        When ChatGPT shipped in November 2022, most people saw “an AI that chats”. Three years later
        AI writes code, runs processes and does research — increasingly as a digital colleague inside
        the workflow. The pace makes most discussion collapse into parameter comparisons and product
        tribalism.
      </p>
      <p>
        What this collection tries to do is put the technology back at the moment it appeared: which
        concrete problem did it solve then? Why this design? What did it cost? And where is the next
        bottleneck? Once those dots connect, a trend stops being a headline and becomes a causal chain
        you can reason about.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is written</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Timeline as the spine.</strong> Official launches, papers and product updates anchor the question of what happened when.</li>
        <li><strong>Questions as the thread.</strong> Every technology answers “what old approach did this replace, and what new problem did it create”.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple viewpoints.</strong> The same technology matters differently to users, application developers, product managers and model builders.</li>
        <li><strong>Checked against practice.</strong> Real deployments are used to test which conclusions only hold on a slide.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Scope and limits</h2>
      <p>
        The main series takes the Claude / Anthropic ecosystem as its primary vantage point, because
        that is the toolchain my team uses most deeply day to day; it also covers key moves from OpenAI
        (GPT-5, Codex), Google (Gemini 3) and DeepSeek (R1). Depth is therefore uneven across chapters —
        read it against your own stack.
      </p>
      <p>
        The enterprise chapters describe internal platforms with company names, product names and exact
        figures removed, keeping only transferable method and milestones. Usage and rate figures in the
        cost essays come from my own billing data; third-party models are converted from public list
        prices. All views are personal and do not represent my employer. Corrections by email are welcome.
      </p>

      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <p>
        The collection keeps growing: more standalone notes on tooling, architecture post-mortems and
        engineering practice. The next series is in preparation.
      </p>

      <h2>Changelog</h2>
      <ul class="update-list">
        <li><time>2026.10.27</time>English editions added for the whole collection; Chinese remains the original.</li>
        <li><time>2026.10.26</time>New essay: AI coding cost optimisation — three months of usage data with the cost formula.</li>
        <li><time>2026.10.25</time>Prologue rewritten as a master outline; MCP and prompt-engineering chapters expanded; references consolidated here.</li>
        <li><time>2026.10.23</time>The collection launched as a structured, extensible site.</li>
        <li><time>2026.06.26</time>New essay: A Day of Agentic Coding.</li>
        <li><time>2026.02.24</time>The series was modularised; the restaurant analogy primer and dual-track comparison were added.</li>
        <li><time>2026.01.27</time>First draft completed, covering 2022.11—2026.01.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Licence and contact</h2>
      <p>
        Text is licensed under <strong>{SERIES.license}</strong>: read and repost freely for
        non-commercial use, with credit and a link back. Images are my own and share the same licence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Feedback, corrections or collaboration: <a href={'mailto:' + SITE.email}>{SITE.email}</a> ·
        <a href={SITE.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> ·
        <a href={SITE.personalSite}>Home</a> ·
        <a href={SITE.base + '/en/ai-evolution/'}>The series</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
